# ifcfilter split rules. Copy to rules.txt and change to suit the models. One rule a line: an action, a space, then its text.
# Lines starting with # are comments.

# Where a thing goes, by text its family's name holds:
# keep-largest  the largest solid is the thing, and the rest is detail inside it, for the hidden file
# physical      transparent, but really there, so not an envelope
# envelope      opaque, but not really there, so an envelope
# hidden        really there, but out of sight, so all of it goes in the hidden file
keep-largest HUME SLAB
physical Grating
physical Stair
envelope Clearance
hidden Pile Cap

# For when the family doesn't say what a thing is, as with a road's meshes, by a property's value: SET.PROPERTY=VALUE
hidden-if Corridor_Mesh_ID.Asset_Identifier_Label=Subbase
envelope-if Corridor_Mesh_ID.Asset_Identifier_Label=Vehicle Envelope

# Where the models keep their asset data. Anything left out is left alone: without site-set and site-id,
# nothing merges by site.
# site-set           a property set holding the site ID; the first is preferred where an element has several
# site-id            the site ID property
# asset-type         corrected to what the site ID starts with
# asset-component    an asset's parts are described as this
# design-set         a property set holding the design tag, for elements with no site ID; first preferred
# design-tag         the design tag property
# mark-set           a property set holding the mark that tells pieces apart; first preferred
# mark               the mark property: elements with a design tag but different marks are apart
# quantity-set       sizes are worked out for a merged asset as a whole in this set, as these three:
# quantity-length, quantity-width, quantity-height
# cull-set           left out of every output once merging has used it
# duplicate-set      what it repeats from other sets on the same elements is left out of it
# project-set        the set holding the next two
# project-long-name  the project's long name, as most of the model has it
# building-name      the building's name, as most of the model has it
# site-name          the site's name, rather than Revit's "Default"
site-set Asset
site-id SiteID
asset-type AssetType
asset-component AssetComponent
design-set Quantities
design-tag DesignTag
mark-set Identity
mark Mark
quantity-set Quantities
quantity-length Length
quantity-width Width
quantity-height Height
cull-set Quantities
duplicate-set Data
project-set Project
project-long-name PackageName
building-name Zone
site-name Site
